   ],
   "start_page": 555,
   "summary": "This section of the monthly report for July 1941 details Japan's military move into southern French Indochina pursuant to an agreement negotiated with Vichy France, securing naval and air bases at Cam Ranh Bay and Saigon. In response, the United States, Great Britain, Canada, and the Netherlands East Indies froze Japanese assets, leading to reciprocal Japanese financial restrictions and intensive domestic press agitation against alleged ABCD encirclement. The report recounts Ambassador Grew's urgent representations over Japanese air raids at Chongqing that severely damaged the USS Tutuila and American mission properties, the Imperial Conference of July 2 defining national policy in light of the German-Soviet hostilities, massive army mobilization and troop movements to Manchuria, the resignation of the second Konoye Cabinet to drop Foreign Minister Yosuke Matsuoka and form the third Konoye Cabinet under Premier Fumimaro Konoe, and the onset of acute economic pressures from foreign trade curtailments.",
   "title": "Report of Conditions in Japan during the Month of July 1941",
   "to": "The Secretary of State"
  },
  {
   "about": "Diplomatic despatch transmitting Report No. 1677 on political, diplomatic, and economic conditions in Japan during August 1941.",
