   "start_page": 583,
   "summary": "Ambassador Joseph C. Grew transmits Report No. 1677 to the Secretary of State reviewing conditions in Japan during August 1941. The report details U.S. representations concerning disparities in the application of freezing regulations, ongoing harassment and seizures of American property in occupied China, the aborted visit of the evacuation liner SS President Coolidge, and provisional proposals on the Fur Seal Convention. It analyzes Japanese reactions to American shipments of aviation fuel to Vladivostok, the joint Roosevelt-Churchill Atlantic Charter declaration, and public revelations concerning Premier Konoe's direct message to President Roosevelt proposing diplomatic negotiations. The document further details Japan's military consolidation in southern Indochina, diplomatic friction with Great Britain over Thailand, the attempted assassination of Minister without Portfolio Baron Kiichiro Hiranuma, and emergency internal measures including national labor conscription and key industry controls.",
   "title": "Despatch No. 5866: Report of Conditions in Japan during the Month of August 1941",
   "to": "The Secretary of State"
  },
